Subject: Store to a cached global without the two property lookups, and give compiled stores their function's strictness

What this does
  - `qjs_aot_put_global` takes the storing function's bytecode. A store to a global that the
    read cache holds (a data property of the global object or of the lexical object, flags
    checked) writes the slot directly; the cell is filled by the engine's store as well as its
    read, so a variable that is only ever assigned is cached too. Everything else is the
    engine's store, which now takes the strictness as an argument (`JS_SetGlobalVarS`).
  - `qjs_aot_call_c` calls a generic builtin before it looks up the per-site table, which only
    the direct-pointer forms need.

Why we need it
  Top-level scripts keep their variables as globals; JS_SetGlobalVar (two hash lookups per
    store) was 6 percent of the hand-written programs. A frameless compiled body has no frame
  the engine can ask whether the code is strict, so its store to an undeclared name created a
  global instead of throwing a ReferenceError ('use strict'; undeclared = 5): the strictness is
  now the function's own, for framed bodies too.

